  title="How to read a research paper efficiently: the three-pass method"
  description="Read a research paper in three passes: skim for the claim, read for the argument, then check the details. What to skip, what to note, and how to keep it."
  question="How do you read a research paper efficiently?"
  answer="Read it in three passes instead of front to back. First skim the title, abstract, figures and conclusion (5–10 minutes) to decide whether it matters. Then read the introduction, method and results for the argument, marking the claims you'd cite. Only for papers central to your work, do a third pass that checks the details, the maths and the references. Write a note at each mark, tied to the passage, so you never have to read it twice."
