  <h2>Pass one: decide whether it matters (5–10 minutes)</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Read the title, abstract, headings, figures and their captions, and the conclusion.</li>
    <li>Glance at the references for papers you already know, and note any <a href={href('glossary/preprint/')}>preprint</a> you may want the published version of.</li>
    <li>Answer five questions: what kind of paper is it, what is the claim, is it relevant to you, is it well founded at a glance, and what are the main contributions?</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Most papers stop here. That is the point: you read more papers well by reading most of them briefly.</p>

  <h2>Pass two: follow the argument (about an hour)</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Read the introduction, method and results. Skip proofs and derivations for now.</li>
    <li>Look hard at figures and tables: axes, error bars, what is compared with what.</li>
    <li>Highlight the claims you might cite and write one line on each: what it says and whether you believe it.</li>
    <li>Mark references you want to follow up: <a href={href('glossary/snowballing/')}>snowballing</a> from them later finds what a search misses.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Pass three: check it (for the papers your work rests on)</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Try to re-derive the key results, or re-run them if the code is available.</li>
    <li>Question every assumption and look for what the paper doesn't test.</li>
    <li>Compare the numbers with your own results or with other papers.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What to note while reading</h2>
  <p>
    A good reading note records the claim, the evidence for it, your judgement, and where on the page it came from.
    That last part is the one people skip, and it's the one that saves you rereading when you write the related-work
    section months later.
  </p>
